This peer carries out the reflexive-domain construction of Exercise 8.25. It builds a finitary projection whose fixed-point domain is universal and isomorphic to its own function space.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`trianglelefteq_iterSys`).** Every neighbourhood system \(V\) is a retract-subdomain of its countable power \(V^\mathbb{N}\).

*Proof.* Embed an element as the constant sequence and project a sequence to its first coordinate. Projection after embedding is the identity, while embedding after projection lies below the identity. ∎

**Theorem (`example_825_embed`).** For every \(V\), one has \(V\mathrel{\trianglelefteq}V^\mathbb{N}\).

*Proof.* This is the preceding constant-sequence projection pair, uniformly quantified over \(V\). ∎

**Definition (`arrowReindexOrderIso`).** A bijection between index types induces an order isomorphism between the corresponding spaces of indexed families by reindexing.

**Definition (`sumArrowOrderIso`).** A family indexed by a disjoint sum is order-isomorphic to a pair of families indexed by the two summands.

**Definition (`interleaveOrderIso`).** A pair of countable families is order-isomorphic to one countable family by interleaving their coordinates.

**Definition (`powProdOrderIso`).** The product \(V^\mathbb{N}\times V^\mathbb{N}\) is order-isomorphic to \(V^\mathbb{N}\), by viewing both powers as sequences and interleaving them.

**Theorem (`pow_prod_isomorphic`).** The product of two copies of the countable power of \(V\) is isomorphic, as a domain, to that countable power.

*Proof.* The interleaving order isomorphism between their element orders induces the required domain isomorphism. ∎

**Theorem (`example_825_pow`).** For every \(V\), \(V^\mathbb{N}\times V^\mathbb{N}\cong V^\mathbb{N}\).

*Proof.* Apply the preceding product-of-powers isomorphism. ∎

**Definition (`isoOfUnique`).** Any two ordered types having exactly one element are order-isomorphic by the unique maps between them.

**Theorem (`uniqueApproximableMapUnitSys`).** There is exactly one approximable endomap of the one-point neighbourhood system.

*Proof.* Every such map has the same action on the sole element, hence equals the identity map. ∎

**Theorem (`uniqueFunSpaceUnitSys`).** The function-space domain from the one-point system to itself has exactly one element.

*Proof.* Its elements correspond to approximable maps of the one-point system, of which there is exactly one. ∎

**Theorem (`isomorphic_of_unique`).** Neighbourhood systems whose element domains each have exactly one element are isomorphic.

*Proof.* Their element orders are related by the unique order isomorphism between singleton ordered types. ∎

**Theorem (`funSpace_unitSys_isomorphic`).** The function space from the one-point system to itself is isomorphic to the one-point system.

*Proof.* Both element domains have exactly one element. ∎

**Theorem (`example_825_unit`).** The one-point domain satisfies \(1^1\cong 1\).

*Proof.* This is the preceding singleton function-space isomorphism. ∎

**Definition (`RDom`).** Let \(R\) be the product \([U\to U]\times([U\to U]\times U)\), the domain of triples consisting of two universal endomaps and one universal element.

**Definition (`RMap`).** For \(c:U\to U\), define a map \(R\to U\) sending \((\psi,\varphi,x)\) to \(c(\varphi(\psi(x)))\).

**Definition (`curryOnceC`).** Currying the preceding map once produces a map from the first function argument to a function of the remaining function argument and universal element.

**Definition (`fixedNbhd_elementIso_fix`).** For a finitary projection \(a\), the element domain of its fixed-neighbourhood system is order-isomorphic to the subtype of elements fixed by \(a\).

**Definition (`lamOp`).** For \(c:U\to U\), twice currying the map \((\psi,\varphi,x)\mapsto c(\varphi(\psi(x)))\) gives an operator sending \(a\) to the curried action of composition around \(a\) and \(c\).

**Definition (`presentationIterU`).** Choose a computable presentation of the countable power \(U^\mathbb{N}\), which is effectively given because \(U\) is effectively given.

**Definition (`cInj`).** Choose the embedding of \(U^\mathbb{N}\) into \(U\) supplied by universality of \(U\).

**Definition (`cProj`).** Choose the corresponding projection from \(U\) onto \(U^\mathbb{N}\).

**Definition (`cCombinator`).** The combinator projection on \(U\) is embedding after projection for the chosen projection pair between \(U^\mathbb{N}\) and \(U\).

**Definition (`TArrow`).** For an endomap \(a\) of \(U\), associate the function space from the fixed-neighbourhood domain of \(a\) to the fixed-neighbourhood domain of the combinator projection.

**Theorem (`cProj_comp_cInj`).** Projecting after embedding \(U^\mathbb{N}\) is the identity.

*Proof.* This is the retraction equation of the chosen universal projection pair. ∎

**Definition (`cElementIso`).** The elements of \(U^\mathbb{N}\) are order-isomorphic to the elements of \(U\) fixed by the combinator projection.

**Definition (`tOpMap`).** For \(c:U\to U\), compose the curried composition operator with the map induced contravariantly and covariantly by the universal decoding and encoding maps, obtaining an endomap of the function-space domain.

**Theorem (`cInj_comp_cProj_le`).** Embedding after projecting \(U\) lies below the identity on \(U\).

*Proof.* This is the order inequality of the chosen universal projection pair. ∎

**Theorem (`isFinitary_cCombinator`).** The combinator projection is finitary.

*Proof.* It arises from a projection pair whose retract is the effectively given domain \(U^\mathbb{N}\). ∎

**Theorem (`isRetraction_cCombinator`).** The combinator projection is idempotent.

*Proof.* In its square, the middle projection-after-embedding composite is the identity. ∎

**Theorem (`isProjection_cCombinator`).** The combinator endomap is a projection.

*Proof.* It is idempotent and lies below the identity. ∎

**Theorem (`isFinitaryProjection_cCombinator`).** The combinator endomap is a finitary projection.

*Proof.* Combine its projection property with its finitarity. ∎

**Theorem (`fixedNbhd_cCombinator_isomorphic`).** The fixed-neighbourhood domain of the combinator projection is isomorphic to \(U^\mathbb{N}\).

*Proof.* Both domains are order-isomorphic to the fixed-point subtype of the same projection. ∎

**Theorem (`hVV_cCombinator`).** The product of two copies of the combinator fixed-neighbourhood domain is isomorphic to that domain.

*Proof.* Transport both factors to \(U^\mathbb{N}\), interleave the two countable powers, and transport back. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_RMap`).** The triple map determined by \(c\) sends \((\psi,\varphi,x)\) to \(c(\varphi(\psi(x)))\).

*Proof.* Evaluate the projections, pairings, and two function applications in its defining composite. ∎

**Theorem (`toApproxMap_lamOp`).** Applying the curried composition operator determined by \(c\) to the function represented by \(a\), then decoding the result, yields the exponential action induced by \(a\) and \(c\).

*Proof.* Successive applications of currying reduce the statement to the explicit triple-value formula, which is exactly composition first by \(a\) and finally by \(c\). ∎

**Theorem (`tOp_tOpMap`).** The fixed-point transformer associated with the operator determined by \(c\), evaluated at \(a\), is the arrow combinator formed from \(a\) and \(c\).

*Proof.* Decode the transformed function-space element and use the preceding identification of the curried operator with the exponential action. ∎

**Theorem (`hT_TArrow`).** For every finitary projection \(a\), the fixed points of its transformed arrow combinator are order-isomorphic to the elements of the function space from the fixed domain of \(a\) to the fixed domain of the combinator projection.

*Proof.* The transformer is the arrow combinator, whose fixed-point theorem identifies its fixed points with the indicated function-space domain. ∎

**Theorem (`ht_tOpMap`).** If \(c\) is a finitary projection, then the transformer determined by \(c\) sends every finitary projection to a finitary projection.

*Proof.* The transformer is the arrow combinator, and arrow combinators preserve finitary projections in both arguments. ∎

**Theorem (`Dsol_isomorphic_funSpace_cCombinator`).** Let \(D\) be the fixed-neighbourhood domain of the least fixed projection generated by the transformer for the combinator projection. Then \(D\cong[D\to C]\), where \(C\) is the combinator fixed-neighbourhood domain.

*Proof.* The transformer preserves finitary projections, so its fixed projection is finitary. Identify the fixed-neighbourhood elements with its fixed points, then apply the fixed-domain theorem and the preceding arrow-family isomorphism. ∎

**Theorem (`U_trianglelefteq_Dsol`).** The universal domain \(U\) is a retract-subdomain of the solution domain \(D\).

*Proof.* Pass successively from \(U\) into \(U^\mathbb{N}\), then into \(C\), then into the constant-function subdomain of \([D\to C]\), and finally transport along \([D\to C]\cong D\). ∎

**Theorem (`exercise_8_25_main`).** The solution domain \(D\) is isomorphic to its own function space \([D\to D]\).

*Proof.* The isomorphism \(D\cong[D\to C]\), together with \(C\times C\cong C\), yields the standard reflexive-domain self-function-space isomorphism. ∎

**Theorem (`exercise_8_25_universal`).** The solution domain \(D\) is universal: \(U\mathrel{\trianglelefteq}D\).

*Proof.* This is the retract chain from the universal domain into the constructed solution domain. ∎
